WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.176 --> 00:00:14.024
There were frightening moments during Roald Amundsen's expedition to conquer the South Pole that tested the character of

2
00:00:14.164 --> 00:00:15.485
each and every man with him.

3
00:00:16.365 --> 00:00:22.249
During one leg of the journey, Amundsen recalled, "We had to work our way across a hard, smooth

4
00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:29.713
slope, which formed an angle of 45 degrees, and ended in a huge bottomless chasm The prospect of

5
00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:34.856
seeing sledge, driver, and dogs slide down sideways and disappear into the abyss was a great one."

6
00:00:35.837 --> 00:00:42.761
But Roald Amundsen had prepared well, so he began his expedition hoping the obstacles nature put before him

7
00:00:42.981 --> 00:00:44.042
would not defeat him.

8
00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:52.267
Born in Borge, Norway, on July 16, 1872, Roald Amundsen decided to be a polar explorer at age

9
00:00:52.327 --> 00:00:59.775
15 after reading about Sir John Franklin, the famous British Arctic explorer who died in 1847 when his

10
00:00:59.835 --> 00:01:01.736
final expedition met with disaster.

11
00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:10.000
Amundsen was attracted by Franklin's adventurous life, but also felt that the doomed expedition was mostly the explorer's

12
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:10.560
own fault.

13
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:17.843
Amundsen believed he could do better at conquering the unknown, so at age 21, he went to sea

14
00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:19.424
to gain command experience.

15
00:01:20.732 --> 00:01:27.334
In 1897, he obtained a position as first mate on the Belgica, a ship commissioned to conduct research

16
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:28.174
in Antarctica.

17
00:01:29.435 --> 00:01:35.636
When the Belgica became frozen in the ice during the trip, Amundsen experienced the brutal hardships a ship's

18
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:38.177
crew can suffer because of an unprepared captain.

19
00:01:39.278 --> 00:01:44.879
In addition to choosing a crew of mostly incompetent and lazy men, the captain hadn't even brought enough

20
00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:47.180
food or warm clothing for an extended voyage.

21
00:01:48.232 --> 00:01:53.614
Then, while facing starvation, the crew was brought to near insanity by a lack of activity through the

22
00:01:53.654 --> 00:01:55.675
endless Antarctic night of the winter months.

23
00:01:56.856 --> 00:02:01.718
It was only when the captain fell ill and the ship's doctor took command that the expedition was

24
00:02:01.778 --> 00:02:09.841
saved. In 1903, Amundsen planned and led the first expedition to obtain scientific data on the magnetic north

25
00:02:09.881 --> 00:02:17.176
pole. He also wanted to find, and became the first to navigate, the elusive Northwest Passage—a possible shipping

26
00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:22.698
route many believed to exist north of Canada that would allow vessels to reach the Pacific Ocean from

27
00:02:22.738 --> 00:02:24.058
the Northern Atlantic Ocean.

28
00:02:25.098 --> 00:02:30.020
This unknown water route had been sought by navigators for centuries, but had never been discovered.

29
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:37.022
On this expedition, Amundsen traveled throughout the area meeting the native people, and he learned from the Inuit

30
00:02:37.062 --> 00:02:38.622
how to survive in the bitter cold.

31
00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:44.804
This knowledge became invaluable when Amundsen's ship became locked in ice for three years.

32
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.765
The Inuit taught him how to handle a dogsled, and Amundsen learned that animal skins kept him warmer

33
00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:52.666
than wool coats.

34
00:02:54.287 --> 00:03:00.653
In September of 1909, Amundsen was in the process of planning another expedition to the North Pole when

35
00:03:00.693 --> 00:03:05.136
he heard that the American explorer Robert Peary had already accomplished that feat.

36
00:03:06.217 --> 00:03:12.283
Immediately, Amundsen decided to challenge the British explorer Robert Scott, who was about to try for the South

37
00:03:12.303 --> 00:03:15.957
Pole. But Amundsen kept his decision secret.

38
00:03:16.917 --> 00:03:22.338
He was afraid his financial backers would want to see if Scott was successful before committing money to

39
00:03:22.378 --> 00:03:23.419
a similar expedition.

40
00:03:24.559 --> 00:03:29.880
So Amundsen didn't even tell his crew about his change of plans until his expedition was well under

41
00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:37.322
way. To increase his odds of success, Amundsen tried to anticipate every difficulty that might arise, and made

42
00:03:37.382 --> 00:03:39.003
plans to avoid or overcome them.

43
00:03:40.003 --> 00:03:45.949
He packed provisions for a much longer expedition than he expected, and assembled a crackerjack crew of eight

44
00:03:46.049 --> 00:03:47.650
mentally and physically strong men.

45
00:03:49.011 --> 00:03:55.195
For his ship, Amundsen chose the Fram, a unique vessel able to withstand the pressures of the ice

46
00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:56.596
that would inevitably surround it.

47
00:03:57.716 --> 00:04:04.380
Lastly, he chose 110 Greenland sled dogs, a breed accustomed to surviving in the frozen wilderness.

48
00:04:05.741 --> 00:04:11.845
On June 7, 1910, the Fram set off from Oslo, Norway, and reached the Ross Ice Shelf on

49
00:04:11.905 --> 00:04:16.268
January 14, 1911—significantly ahead of Robert Scott.

50
00:04:17.288 --> 00:04:23.992
The crew built Framheim, a base camp of tents and snow buildings three kilometers from shore, and during

51
00:04:24.012 --> 00:04:30.576
the next three months, they transported supplies to three depots about one hundred kilometers apart along the route

52
00:04:30.616 --> 00:04:31.457
to the South Pole.

53
00:04:32.598 --> 00:04:38.233
On April 22, when the sun was visible for the last time until spring, the men at Framheim

54
00:04:38.293 --> 00:04:40.295
settled into the long Antarctic winter.

55
00:04:41.416 --> 00:04:48.242
To help them cope with total darkness and temperatures that would reach as low as -55 °C, Amundsen

56
00:04:48.282 --> 00:04:50.143
planned definite routines for his crew.

57
00:04:51.725 --> 00:04:58.971
For the next four months, each crew member had chores: hunting seals, cleaning, cooking, charting scientific observations, fueling

58
00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:00.953
lamps and heaters, and caring for the dogs.

59
00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:04.596
At night they played cards and darts and even did needlework.

60
00:05:05.588 --> 00:05:13.454
The sun finally rose again on August 24, and preparations resumed so that on October 19, 1911, Amundsen

61
00:05:13.554 --> 00:05:16.276
and four of his men finally set off for the South Pole.

62
00:05:17.337 --> 00:05:23.502
The other four remained behind to make scientific observations on King Edward VII Land, a peninsula on the

63
00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:24.843
edge of the Ross Ice Shelf.

64
00:05:26.144 --> 00:05:34.325
Amundsen's group proceeded toward the pole with four sleds and fifty-two dogs; as temperatures ranged from -21 °C

65
00:05:34.385 --> 00:05:39.246
to -30 °C, the dogs pulled the sleds while the men skied alongside.

66
00:05:40.327 --> 00:05:45.908
The men and the sleds traveled approximately 17 kilometers a day on flat land and gentle rises.

67
00:05:46.989 --> 00:05:51.130
They stopped and built snow beacons from blocks of ice to guide them on the way back.

68
00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:58.865
The men's food consisted of biscuits, chocolate and dried milk for hot chocolate, and pemmican—a substance made of

69
00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:01.386
dried meat, fat, and fruits used by the Inuit.

70
00:06:02.547 --> 00:06:08.130
Soon, however, they entered a landscape of rugged mountains, ice blocks, shallow cracks, and deep chasms.

71
00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:17.394
The dogs trudged up peaks and glaciers ranging from 610 to 3,375 meters high, pulling the sleds plus

72
00:06:17.414 --> 00:06:18.295
the men on skis.

73
00:06:19.115 --> 00:06:23.898
At times, the steepness of the slope was so great that the heavy sleds had to ascend one

74
00:06:23.938 --> 00:06:27.400
at a time with twice as many dogs pulling each sled to make it up the mountain.

75
00:06:28.752 --> 00:06:32.775
On the descent, ropes were wrapped around the sleds' runners to slow them down.

76
00:06:33.876 --> 00:06:37.138
Blizzards delayed the group, making the journey more difficult.

77
00:06:38.019 --> 00:06:45.184
But on December 14, 1911, after almost two months, all five explorers finally planted the Norwegian flag at

78
00:06:45.224 --> 00:06:49.107
the South Pole on a plateau more than 3,000 meters high.

79
00:06:50.468 --> 00:06:55.252
Leaving behind a small tent, a letter to the king of Norway, and a note to Robert Scott,

80
00:06:55.868 --> 00:07:00.811
the men set off three days later on the return trip with only two sleds, eating the food

81
00:07:00.851 --> 00:07:02.012
they had stored along the way.

82
00:07:03.192 --> 00:07:10.677
On January 25, 1912, the five men and their eleven surviving dogs reached Framheim, 99 days after they

83
00:07:10.697 --> 00:07:11.337
had started out.

84
00:07:12.338 --> 00:07:17.481
Robert Scott reached the pole about a month later; tragically, he had chosen a starting point farther from

85
00:07:17.521 --> 00:07:22.424
the pole than Amundsen and used ponies to carry supplies instead of hardy sled dogs.

86
00:07:23.648 --> 00:07:28.591
When his expedition experienced terrible weather on their return, Scott and his men froze to death.

87
00:07:29.872 --> 00:07:36.377
Roald Amundsen never stopped exploring, and in 1925, he took two planes as far north as anyone had

88
00:07:36.437 --> 00:07:36.957
ever flown.

89
00:07:38.038 --> 00:07:43.261
The next year, he flew over the North Pole in an airship and verified that the North Pole

90
00:07:43.301 --> 00:07:44.622
was made of nothing but ice.

91
00:07:45.743 --> 00:07:52.848
Unfortunately, however, on June 18, 1928, while attempting to rescue a group of lost explorers in the Arctic,

92
00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.002
Amundsen's plane disappeared and he was never seen again.

93
00:07:57.062 --> 00:08:02.465
But his memory is kept alive by all the places that have been named for him: among those,

94
00:08:02.946 --> 00:08:08.389
the Amundsen Sea, the Amundsen Glacier, and at the moon's south pole, the Amundsen Crater.
